      "note_id": "borrowed_from_polish",
      "kind": "helpful_transfer",
      "text": "English and Polish are distant cousins, so look-alike words are rare. The first words here are English words that came from Polish, mostly food, dance and money. Some came by a longer road: babka by way of Yiddish, mazurka by way of German and Russian, and horde by way of French and German (Polish took it from Russian, which took it from a Turkic language). The last ones are old shared words (brother, sister, mother, new, two, three, water, mouse, nose, wolf): true relatives from a common ancestor, not borrowings, and thousands of years have changed how they sound.",
      "pairs": [
        {"from": "pierogi", "to": "pierogi", "to_ipa": "pjɛˈrɔɡi", "to_meaning": "filled dumplings"},
        {"from": "kielbasa", "to": "kiełbasa", "to_ipa": "kjɛwˈbasa", "to_meaning": "sausage"},
        {"from": "zloty", "to": "złoty", "to_ipa": "ˈzwɔtɨ", "to_meaning": "golden; the Polish currency"},
        {"from": "babka", "to": "babka", "to_ipa": "ˈbapka", "to_meaning": "grandmother; also a yeast cake"},
        {"from": "paczki", "to": "pączki", "to_ipa": "ˈpɔ̃tʂki", "to_meaning": "filled doughnuts"},
        {"from": "mazurka", "to": "mazurek", "to_ipa": "maˈzurɛk", "to_meaning": "a Polish folk dance"},
        {"from": "horde", "to": "horda", "to_ipa": "ˈxɔrda", "to_meaning": "a horde; also a gang"},
        {"from": "bigos", "to": "bigos", "to_ipa": "ˈbiɡɔs", "to_meaning": "hunter's stew"},
        {"from": "brother", "to": "brat", "to_ipa": "brat"},
